course: dictionary
lesson: 11690
title: Attainment and achievement
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Attainment is a formal word, so you will meet it more in school reports and official writing than in chatting with friends. It means reaching a goal, or getting something after a lot of effort. «Her attainment in reading was high, so her teacher gave her a harder book.» Here it means how well she has learned and achieved.
explain: Look for clues. Words like target, high, levels and goal show what attainment is about. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try a simpler word, such as results, in the sentence instead. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
explain: In everyday talk people say results or what you've achieved instead of attainment. In school it is a stiff, official word, so it is worth knowing both. When you are not sure, a dictionary will settle it.
fact: Attainment means reaching a goal or getting something after effort.
fact: In school, attainment means how much a pupil has learned or achieved, often measured by tests and grades.
fact: «Attainment target» and «high attainment» are common school phrases.
fact: «The attainment of the objective» is a formal way of saying reaching the goal.
fact: An attainment is a skill or achievement that someone has gained.
exercise: «Her attainment in maths was high, with top marks in every test.» What does attainment mean here? | The maths book she lost | *How well she has learned and achieved | How often she goes to the library | The maths lesson she missed last Tuesday
exercise: The school set a new attainment target for Year 5 reading. What is an attainment target? | A list of the school dinners | A playground game | *A goal that pupils aim to reach | The name of a new classroom
exercise: Which words are the best everyday swap for attainment? | Rain and snow | Holidays and breaks | *Results or what you've achieved | Tiredness after a long day
test: «After months of training, the climbers celebrated the attainment of their goal.» What does attainment mean here? | Sleeping in a tent | Losing the way down the mountain | *Reaching the goal after hard work | A new pair of boots
test: Which of these is a common school phrase using attainment? | Attainment weather | Bags of attainment | *Levels of attainment | A time of attainment
exam: Which sentence uses attainment to mean how much a pupil has achieved? | *The report showed her high attainment in writing | The cat had attainment for dinner | The attainment of the football was very loud | She put attainment on her toast
